## Scope and evidence

This review uses the 4 October 2026 export of ticket #4471. Its overlapping export pages
contain repeated report IDs. Count each ID once; identical wording with different IDs
still represents separate reports. The table below computes its counts from the supplied
data and tenant directory. It describes this snapshot, not the desk's current queue.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Separate the fixes

The shared parser reads dates month first even for tenants whose locale writes the day
first. An intended 7 April can become 4 July. This is the majority cause in #4471, seen
through app, email, and booking-v2 forms. Fix date interpretation at the parser boundary
using the tenant's locale, with tests for ambiguous and unambiguous dates.

A separate booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` to day-first tenants, while interpreting
their input in the tenant's day-first order. Users following that hint can create a
different date from the one intended. Correct the hint and align its examples and validation
with the form's parser. A shared-parser fix alone does not establish that this is fixed.

Use tenant locale, booking-form version, and report channel to determine these groups.
Browser locale and intake tags are guesses, not the tenant's settings. Some intake tags
disagree with the computed cause. Source: `src/lib/kb/causes.ts`, `locales.ts`, and tenant
JSON files; the complete report membership is in `evidence/analysis.json`.

<Callout>
Two confirmed failure paths need separate engineering tickets and separate verification.
The old page's “one issue, one fix queued” conclusion does not follow from its own table.
These are investigations and proposed fixes; this review does not establish that either
fix has shipped or passed verification.
</Callout>

## Reports that still need a closer look

The desk notes identify R-41202, R-41209, R-41236, R-41344, and R-41620 as one-day-move
reports. The supplied classifier places all five in the parser group by tenant and channel;
the table footnotes that classification. Their descriptions include a one-day difference,
a June-only problem, an invite preceding a booking, and different dates in Lisbon and
Toronto. Those observations do not prove a month/day swap or a single additional cause.

Keep these five inside the computed totals, flag their cause as provisional, and obtain
the original entered value, stored timestamp, intended date, timezone, and generated invite
before confirming or splitting them. Do not add a timezone bug as an established diagnosis
or count those five twice. The two tickets account for the entire exported set under the
supplied classification, with this limitation explicit.

## What the queue shows

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reception and merge timestamps describe different events. Early reports waited until a
later merge batch; a merge spike is not the onset of a new incident or evidence of a fix.
Reports continued after that batch. Reconcile new arrivals before declaring the live queue
fully covered; this export ends on 4 October.

## Change the triage rule

[Decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause) proposes replacing subject-only deduplication.
The same subject can conceal different failure paths, while different subjects can describe
the same path. Confirm the cause using tenant settings and channel; leave uncertain reports
unmerged pending investigation. Preserve decision 0001 as history.

